    "content": "\"For each item below, fetch its source and check that the recorded quote appears on the page and supports the item. A card item is a field of the plan in the producer's words, with the sources it cites: check each factual claim in it against those sources, and any value it carries against the page; what the plan will build, measure or predict needs no source, but a factual claim with no source is UNSUPPORTED. For a cost input, check that the page states the number for the setting given; whether it applies to this plan is not yours to judge. When the page states a different number for a num or rate item, give it as page_value. Decide only from the pages you fetch and the text below; do not read files. Return one status per item, under its ref: SUPPORTED, PARTIAL, UNSUPPORTED, CONTRADICTED or UNREACHABLE, with the page sentence you relied on.\nItems, one per line:\np1:c1: MatterGen is a fine-tunable crystal foundation model, and its released fine-tuned checkpoints cover only chemistry, symmetry, magnetic density, band gap, and bulk modulus — there is no ionic-conductivity or transport-property checkpoint, so the card's conductivity-conditioned adapter is not yet built. | source: https://github.com/microsoft/mattergen | recorded quote: \\\"We provide checkpoints of an unconditional base version of MatterGen as well as fine-tuned models for these properties: * `mattergen_base`: unconditional base model trained on Alex-MP-20 * `mp_20_base`: unconditional base model trained on MP-20 * `chemical_system`: fine-tuned model conditioned on chemical system * `space_group`: fine-tuned model conditioned on space group * `dft_mag_density`: fine-tuned model conditioned on magnetic density from DFT * `dft_band_gap`: fine-tuned model conditioned on band gap from DFT * `ml_bulk_modulus`: fine-tuned model conditioned on bulk modulus from ML predictor * `dft_mag_density_hhi_score`: fine-tuned model jointly conditioned on magnetic density from DFT and HHI score * `chemical_system_energy_above_hull`: fine-tuned model jointly conditioned on chemical system and energy above hull from DFT\\\"\np1:c2: Their battery solid-electrolyte discovery today is large-scale screening (32.6M candidates filtered down), not conductivity-conditioned generation, and it ends with synthesis plus a measured conductivity check of the top NaxLi3-xYCl6 candidates. | source: https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2401.04070 | recorded quote: \\\"By focusing on solid-state electrolytes for battery applications, our discovery pipeline further identified 18 promising candidates with new compositions, and 1 rediscovered a decade's worth of collective knowledge in the field as a byproduct. By employing around one thousand virtual machines (VMs) in the cloud, this process took less than 80 hours.\\\"\np1:c3: The programme has explicitly stated that the next step beyond screening is direct property-conditioned generation of materials (pointing to MatterGen), which is exactly the step this card targets. | source: https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2401.04070 | recorded quote: \\\"Future optimization of the workflow beyond screening will be based on the direct generation of materials with given properties, such as the method recently demonstrated by MatterGen [41].\\\"\np1:c4: The Azure Quantum Elements product leader describes the team's stated ambition as generative battery-materials discovery from property prompts. | source: https://news.microsoft.com/source/features/innovation/how-ai-and-hpc-are-speeding-up-scientific-discovery/ | recorded quote: \\\"The vision we are working toward is generative materials where I can ask for list of new battery compounds with my desired attributes, Baker says.\\\"\np1:done: MatterGen has been fine-tuned on magnetic density, band gap, and bulk modulus (never ionic conductivity), and their battery electrolyte result came from screening 32M candidates rather than conductivity-conditioned generation — their own future-tense plan confirms the generative-transport step is not yet done. | source: https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2401.04070 | recorded quote: \\\"Future optimization of the workflow beyond screening will be based on the direct generation of materials with given properties, such as the method recently demonstrated by MatterGen [41].\\\"\np2:c1: Their battery workflow uses MatterGen for structure sampling plus MatterSim/DFT screening, and generation is conditioned on thermodynamic stability (convex-hull energy), not on ionic conductivity. | source: https://doi.org/10.1002/batt.202500309 | recorded quote: \\\"Our LIB material discovery workflow uses MatterGen for structure sampling, [12] MatterSim for initial screening of stable materials, [4] and DFT for final validation.\\\"\np2:c2: The scale of their generative campaign is tens of thousands of samples (32,600), narrowed to 91 stable Li-containing materials and finally two cathode candidates — so the card's 1k-100k candidate axis sits directly on their scale but targets electrolytes/conductivity, which they did not. | source: https://doi.org/10.1002/batt.202500309 | recorded quote: \\\"Here, we first conditionally sample structures with a target convex hull energy of 0.03 eV/atom with respect to MP data within the MatterGen framework, and 32,600 structures are generated in this process.\\\"\np2:c3: Their electrolyte-specific use of a foundation model is property PREDICTION (fine-tuning MACE-MP-0b2-large on a ~400k halide database), not generation — the inverse-design generation they did produced cathodes, leaving conductivity-conditioned electrolyte generation unclaimed. | source: https://orbit.dtu.dk/en/publications/domain-specific-dataset-enable-accurate-mlips-for-disordered-hali/ | recorded quote: \\\"We demonstrate the utility of the database by fine-tuning the MACE-MP-0b2-large foundation model: the energy root-mean-square error (RMSE) decreased from 154.8 to 10.0 meV atom -1 and the all-force-component RMSE from 168.3 to 39.0 meV Å-1 on a held-out test set, confirming that domain-specific training data is necessary for structurally disordered halide configurations.\\\"\np2:c4: The generative chapter of their 'inverse design of solid electrolytes' thesis delivers cathode candidates, not conductivity-conditioned electrolytes — confirming the card's step is the unfilled gap in their own programme. | source: https://orbit.dtu.dk/en/publications/deep-generative-models-for-inverse-design-of-solid-electrolytes/ | recorded quote: \\\"The third contribution moves from screening known materials to inventing new ones. A generative AI model designed for crystals is used to propose tens of thousands of new candidate structures, which are then filtered through a multi-step pipeline combining a universal machine-learning potential and density functional theory. Starting from 32,600 generated structures, the pipeline narrows the field to 91 stable, novel, lithium-containing materials and ultimately identifies two previously unreported candidates that look promising as cathodes.\\\"\np2:done: They have run MatterGen generation for battery materials (cathodes, conditioned on stability) and fine-tuned foundation models for halide electrolyte property prediction, but have not fine-tuned a generative model on ionic conductivity to generate electrolytes — that exact experiment is absent from both their paper and thesis. | source: https://orbit.dtu.dk/en/publications/deep-generative-models-for-inverse-design-of-solid-electrolytes/ | recorded quote: \\\"Chapter 5 - Generative Design of Li-Ion Cathodes This chapter reports the generative discovery of cathode materials for lithium-\\\"\nReturn only JSON: {\\\"items\\\": [{\\\"ref\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"status\\\": \\\"SUPPORTED | PARTIAL | UNSUPPORTED | CONTRADICTED | UNREACHABLE\\\", \\\"page_quote\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"note\\\": \\\"\\\"}]}\""
