    "content": "     Deep Generative Models for Inverse Design of Solid Electrolytes - Welcome to DTU Research Database            \n\n[Skip to main navigation](#main-navigation) [Skip to search](#main-search) [Skip to main content](#main-content)\n\n[Welcome to DTU Research Database Home ![Welcome to DTU Research Database Logo](/skin/headerImage/)](/en/) \n\n-   [About DTU Orbit   Link opens in a new tab](https://www.bibliotek.dtu.dk/en/publishing/research-publishing/research-output/dtu-orbit)\n\nSearch content at Welcome to DTU Research Database \n\n-   [Home](/en/)\n-   [Profiles](/en/persons/)\n-   [Research units](/en/organisations/)\n-   [Publications](/en/publications/)\n-   [Activities](/en/activities/)\n-   [Projects](/en/projects/)\n-   [Prizes](/en/prizes/)\n-   [Press/Media](/en/clippings/)\n-   [Datasets](/en/datasets/)\n\n# Deep Generative Models for Inverse Design of Solid Electrolytes\n\n-   Chiku Parida\n\n-   [Atomic Scale Materials Modelling](https://orbit.dtu.dk/en/organisations/atomic-scale-materials-modelling/)\n-   [Department of Energy Conversion and Storage](https://orbit.dtu.dk/en/organisations/department-of-energy-conversion-and-storage/)\n\nResearch output: Book/Report › Ph.D. thesis\n\n59 Downloads (Orbit)\n\n-   [Overview](/en/publications/deep-generative-models-for-inverse-design-of-solid-electrolytes/)\n-   [Fingerprint](/en/publications/deep-generative-models-for-inverse-design-of-solid-electrolytes/fingerprints/)\n-   [Projects (1)](/en/publications/deep-generative-models-for-inverse-design-of-solid-electrolytes/projects/)\n\n## Abstract\n\nLithium-ion batteries underpin the global shift away from fossil fuels, but their energy density and safety remain limited by the use of flammable liquid electrolytes and a narrow set of cathode materials. Replacing the liquid with a solid-state electrolyte and pairing it with new high-voltage cathodes is widely seen as the most promising path forward. The challenge is the size of the search space: the number of possible compositions, dopants, and crystal structures is far too large to test one by one in the laboratory or by traditional simulation. This thesis develops a computational pipeline that combines first-principles calculations, machine-learning inter atomic potentials (MLIPs), molecular dynamics, and generative artificial intelligence to accelerate the discovery of both solid electrolytes and cathodes for next-generation batteries. The work is organized around three contributions, each corresponding to one published or in-preparation paper.  \n  \nThe first contribution is a large, open computational database for halide solid electrolytes of the Li MX family, where M is a metal and X is a halogen (F, Cl, or Br). The database contains roughly 400,000 structures across more than 18,000 unique compositions, generated by systematically varying the metal, the halogen, and the type and amount of doping. Using this dataset to fine-tune a state-of-the-art foundation model reduces its prediction error on halide configurations by more than an order of magnitude, turning a generic model into one that is reliable for this specific class of materials. The dataset is made publicly available as a resource for the broader battery and machine learning community.  \n  \nThe second contribution uses trained MLIP to study how a particular type of doping replacing a small fraction of the metal with a higher-valence cation, which forces the formation of lithium vacancies controls how easily lithium ions move through the electrolyte. Through a combination of thermodynamic screening and large-scale molecular dynamics across a wide composition space, the study connects the choice of host metal and the level of doping to the underlying ion-transport mechanism, and outlines design principles for selecting compositions where vacancy engineering is expected to be beneficial. It also shows that doping helps in some hosts but hurts in others, and explains why there is an optimal vacancy concentration beyond which the lithium network breaks apart and ion transport collapses. This converts a long-standing rule of thumb into a quantitative design principle.  \n  \nThe third contribution moves from screening known materials to inventing new ones. A generative AI model designed for crystals is used to propose tens of thousands of new candidate structures, which are then filtered through a multi-step pipeline combining a universal machine-learning potential and density functional theory. Starting from 32,600 generated structures, the pipeline narrows the field to 91 stable, novel, lithium-containing materials and ultimately identifies two previously unreported candidates that look promising as cathodes. The same analysis maps the strengths and limitations of the generative model, clarifying when it can be used directly and when domain specific data is still needed.  \n  \nTaken together, these three contributions show that combining curated data, machine learning potentials, and generative models can move battery materials discovery beyond traditional trial-and-error screening, delivering concrete candidates on both sides of the cell the electrolyte and the cathode and providing a template for similar tasks in other materials classes.\n\nOriginal language\n\nEnglish\n\nPlace of Publication\n\nKgs. Lyngby\n\nPublisher\n\n[Technical University of Denmark](#)\n\nNumber of pages\n\n164\n\nPublication status\n\nPublished - 2026\n\n## UN SDGs\n\nThis output contributes to the following UN [Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs)](https://www.un.org/sustainabledevelopment/sustainable-development-goals/)\n\n1.  ![SDG 7 - Affordable and Clean Energy](/assets/sdg_icons/affordable_and_clean_energy-b8e39c169139faf6df7199566119c3c0.svg \"SDG 7 - Affordable and Clean Energy\")\n    \n    SDG 7 Affordable and Clean Energy\n    \n\n## Access to Document\n\n-   [Fulltext](/files/458182149/PhD_Thesis_CPF.pdf)Final published version, 16.7 MB\n    \n\n## OpenUrl avai