    "reasoning_content": "Let me go through each item:\n\n**p1:c1**: MatterGen is a fine-tunable crystal foundation model, and its released fine-tuned checkpoints cover only chemistry, symmetry, magnetic density, band gap, and bulk modulus — there is no ionic-conductivity or transport-property checkpoint.\n\nSource: github.com/microsoft/mattergen. The README says: \"We provide checkpoints of an unconditional base version of MatterGen as well as fine-tuned models for these properties: mattergen_base, mp_20_base, chemical_system, space_group, dft_mag_density, dft_band_gap, ml_bulk_modulus, dft_mag_density_hhi_score, chemical_system_energy_above_hull.\"\n\nThe recorded quote matches the page. Checkpoints: chemical system (chemistry), space group (symmetry), magnetic density, band gap, bulk modulus. Indeed no ionic conductivity checkpoint. The claim \"there is no ionic-conductivity or transport-property checkpoint\" — the page lists all checkpoints and none is ionic conductivity. That's supported by the complete list on the page. Also \"MatterGen is a fine-tunable crystal foundation model\" — page: \"a generative model for inorganic materials design across the periodic table that can be fine-tuned to steer the generation towards a wide range of property constraints\". Supported. Status: SUPPORTED.\n\n**p1:c2**: Their battery solid-electrolyte discovery today is large-scale screening (32.6M candidates filtered down), not conductivity-conditioned generation, and it ends with synthesis plus a measured conductivity check of the top NaxLi3-xYCl6 candidates.\n\nSource: arxiv 2401.04070 (abstract page). The abstract says: \"navigate through more than 32 million candidates and predict around half a million potentially stable materials. By focusing on solid-state electrolytes for battery applications, our discovery pipeline further identified 18 promising candidates with new compositions... We then synthesized and experimentally characterized the structures and conductivities of