    "reasoning_content": "Let me review each item against the fetched content:\n\n**p1:c1** — GitHub MatterGen README. The recorded quote matches the page verbatim: \"We provide checkpoints of an unconditional base version of MatterGen as well as fine-tuned models for these properties:\" with the list including mattergen_base, mp_20_base, chemical_system, space_group, dft_mag_density, dft_band_gap, ml_bulk_modulus, dft_mag_density_hhi_score, chemical_system_energy_above_hull. The claim: \"MatterGen is a fine-tunable crystal foundation model\" — page says \"a generative model for inorganic materials design across the periodic table that can be fine-tuned to steer the generation towards a wide range of property constraints.\" Supported. \"released fine-tuned checkpoints cover only chemistry, symmetry, magnetic density, band gap, and bulk modulus\" — the checkpoint list: chemical system (chemistry), space group (symmetry), mag density, band gap, bulk modulus, plus two jointly-conditioned (mag density + HHI score, chemical system + energy above hull). HHI score and energy above hull aren't listed in the claim's \"only\" list. Hmm — the claim says fine-tuned checkpoints cover ONLY chemistry, symmetry, magnetic density, band gap, and bulk modulus. But the page also lists `dft_mag_density_hhi_score` (mag density + HHI score) and `chemical_system_energy_above_hull` (chem system + energy above hull). These are combinations that still fall within chemistry/symmetry/etc.? HHI score is not chemistry per se; energy above hull is not in the list either. The claim's \"only\" enumeration is slightly incomplete — the two jointly-conditioned checkpoints carry conditioning dims (HHI score, energy above hull) outside the named set. However, the joint ones are combinations: dft_mag_density_hhi_score = mag density + HHI; chemical_system_energy_above_hull = chemical system + energy above hull. So energy above hull is a new conditioning property not in the \"only\" list. Strictly, that makes the \"only\" claim slightly inaccurate. The core claim — no ionic-conductivity or transport-property checkpoint — is fully supported: nothing conductivity-related in the list. Is this SUPPORTED or PARTIAL? The instruction: \"check each factual claim in it against those sources, and any value it carries against the page\". The \"only\" enumeration omits energy_above_hull and hhi_score as conditioning properties. That's a minor incompleteness. I'd call it PARTIAL with a note, or SUPPORTED if I consider HHI score a measure of chemical supply risk and energy above hull tied to chemical system... No, energy above hull is a stability property, not chemistry. Strict reading: the released fine-tuned checkpoints' conditioning properties go beyond the five listed. But the main thrust (no conductivity checkpoint, adapter not yet built) is supported. I think PARTIAL is the fair call with a note about the two jointly-conditioned checkpoints introducing HHI score and energy above hull beyond the named list. Hmm, but one could argue the jointly-conditioned checkpoints are fine-tuned models for mag density and chem system \"plus\" extra. The set of distinct properties: chemical system, space group, mag density, band gap, bulk modulus, HHI score, energy above hull. The claim says \"only chemistry, symmetry, magnetic density, band gap, and bulk modulus\". Energy above hull and HHI score are missing from that list. So PARTIAL.\n\nWait — let me reconsider. Is this the kind of nuance worth flagging? The task says: \"check each factual claim in it against those sources\". The factual claim \"cover only [5 properties]\" is slightly contradicted by the two joint checkpoints. I'll go PARTIAL with the note, still affirming no conductivity checkpoint exists.\n\n**p1:c2** — arXiv 2401.04070. Recorded quote: \"By focusing on solid-state electrolytes for battery applications, our discovery pipeline further identified 18 promising candidates with new compositions, and 1 rediscovered a decade's